<p>Angela Rayner, Britain’s former deputy prime minister, said HM Revenue and Customs cleared her of deliberate wrongdoing, carelessness and tax avoidance after investigating unpaid stamp duty linked to a property purchase. The Guardian reported that Rayner settled £40,000 in unpaid stamp duty after initially paying a lower rate and did not pay a penalty after the investigation. Prime Minister Keir Starmer faces pressure over his leadership. Politico reported that Health Secretary Wes Streeting was widely expected to launch a leadership challenge as soon as Thursday; Rayner declined to rule out standing if a contest takes place but said she would not trigger one. Rayner resigned last September as deputy prime minister and housing secretary after acknowledging that she had underpaid stamp duty on a flat near Brighton. Asked whether Starmer should step aside, Rayner said he would “have to reflect on that”.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/CI_YlJoA79vluAuczd4EVag7Qg-5wJo35SYgXKT6vhGlfiGoqHMrBEpIvMGSaTzWq2adYir7SYYz3O0OBh6bnbTmYuD-MkjNMK3lqx6-z8AikwrR9WIs20JZL2iHPdrJ7EMnjrFgS6RPNa3hneY4OVbXd4GVEUCaMCC3O4pyApFWzB4qftJGY1L_3_5inJXS' alt='Angela Rayner last year. She has been seen as a potential successor to Prime Minister Keir Starmer.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Ethics finding: The London Economic reported that Rayner referred herself to the prime minister’s ethics adviser, who concluded that she “acted with integrity” but had breached the ministerial code.</li><li>Party position: Politico described Rayner as a standard-bearer of Labour’s soft left, with support among grassroots members.</li><li>Labour critique: Rayner said the pace of change had not been sufficient for voters to see and that mistakes had blown Labour off course.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Angela Rayner: Rayner said experts had advised her to pay stamp duty at the standard rate because she did not own another property or hold a personal financial interest in the trust set up fo